================================================ Step 1 ================================================

Your 90-second answer

“I worked on the customer-facing side of collections and cheque recovery. Retailers receive WhatsApp notifications about their payments, and those messages can contain generated financial documents.

There are three main flows. First is normal collection — once a salesman's payments are fully verified, retailers receive a payment receipt showing what they paid and their remaining outstanding. Second, when a cheque bounces, we send a proforma invoice for the ₹500 bounce charge. Third, when that charge is actually recovered, we generate a GST tax invoice through ClearTax with an IRN and signed QR code.

The collection flow had an interesting consistency problem. Cashier verification happens payment by payment, and a collection can become fully verified over multiple days. So sending a notification on every verification event could expose incomplete outstanding amounts or send duplicates. The design uses an event as a signal and derives the actual eligibility from current database state. A cron sweep provides a fallback if the event path fails.

The hardest technical problem was GST calculation. The ₹500 bounce charge is GST-inclusive, but the government portal validates both the individual 9% CGST/SGST calculations and the final total. Simple gross / 1.18 rounding can create a one-paisa mismatch and reject the invoice. So I implemented a bounded Decimal-based search around candidate taxable values and selected one satisfying both constraints exactly.

Kafka then decouples the eligibility decision from document generation, S3, ClearTax and WATI.”


================================================ Step 2 ================================================

“Explain the collection notification flow.”

“There are two paths.

The primary design was event-driven. When the cashier verifies a payment, we publish a Kafka event containing the salesman and collection date.

But the event doesn't contain the invoices to send. It only says: something changed for this salesman on this date.

The consumer then queries the current database state and checks whether that salesman-day is actually eligible.

We also have a cron sweep running the same eligibility logic. Its job is to recover cases where the event wasn't delivered or wasn't processed successfully.

Both paths converge on the same eligibility query and notification log, so whichever path gets there first sends the notification and the other becomes a no-op.”


================================================ Step 3 ================================================
If they ask:

“Why not just send the notification directly from the verify API?”

Your answer:

“Because verification isn't equivalent to completion.

The application can call verification multiple times, and a collection can have multiple payments verified independently. If I use the individual verification event as the trigger and trust its payload, I can send a receipt while the collection is still partially verified.

So the event only identifies the affected salesman-day. The consumer derives eligibility from current state.”

Then example:

“Suppose there are four payments. Two are verified today and two three days later. We don't want two receipts. The first two days fail the all-verified condition. Once the fourth payment is verified, the sweep sees the entire salesman-day as complete and sends one receipt containing all four payments.”

================================================ Step 4 ================================================

“There are two possible producers of the same notification — the event path and the fallback sweep — so idempotency has to be shared.

The eligibility query checks for an existing successful notification using NOT EXISTS.

We also create a pending notification log before sending and update it after the provider call.

So the database log is effectively the coordination point between the two paths.”


================================================ Step 5 ================================================

If the interviewer asks about extensibility:

“The consumer receives different notification keys, and NotificationService maps those keys to strategy-specific payload builders and shapes.

Collection notifications operate on a list and aggregate by customer; cheque notifications operate on a single cheque.

Adding a notification therefore doesn't require modifying the producer-consumer pipeline — it's essentially a new key, payload builder and strategy mapping.”

================================================ Step 5 ================================================

⭐ The bug you SHOULD volunteer

“There's one real bug I'd fix first. In the Kafka consumer's exception handler, the code logs the exception and still commits the message.

That means a processing failure can mark the Kafka message consumed, so Kafka won't retry it and there's no DLQ.

For collection notifications, the cron fallback partially protects us because a failed send leaves the notification unsuccessful and the next sweep can pick it up.

But cheque notifications don't have that fallback, so a failed event can genuinely be lost.

I'd stop committing failed messages and add a dead-letter topic with controlled retries.”


